test_lagrit_upstream -- LaGriT's OWN test suite, run from this suite
=======================================================================

WHAT IT IS (Oct 16 2026)

  LaGriT ships its own tests in its source tree: test/level01 (43
  tests, the ones GitHub runs on every pull request), test/level02
  (Exodus) and test/level03 (development, never run automatically).
  Each test is a folder with input.lgi and reference/outx3dgen, the
  log a correct run writes. This suite runs those tests on the build
  under test and judges them, so LaGriT's own tests are part of every
  run_all.sh. It is the check a pull request into lanl/LaGriT will get.

  The tests are NOT copied into this suite (about 105 MB, and they
  change with LaGriT): they are read from ../../level01 in this checkout.

QUICK START

  run_upstream_tests.sh                    (about 30 s with the pictures)
  LEVEL2=1 run_upstream_tests.sh           (also the Exodus tests;
                                             needs a build with Exodus)
  open logs/summary.html

  The tests come from (first that exists; printed at the start):
    $LAGRIT_TESTS            a test/ folder given by hand
    ../..                    test/ of this checkout (usual case)
    $LAGDEV/test             another LaGriT source tree
    <lagrit's folder>/../test

FILES

  run_upstream_tests.sh  runner (POSIX sh): copies each test folder
                         (inputs only) into work/<test>, runs
                         lagrit -log logx3dgen -out outx3dgen < input.lgi
                         there (as LaGriT's runtests.py does), then
                         calls the checker. The source tree is never
                         written to.
  check_upstream.py      checker (Python 3, standard library): judges
                         every test; writes logs/table.txt and
                         logs/summary.html. Its opening comment defines
                         every check exactly.
  draw_meshes.py         the pictures: one SVG per AVS mesh file
                         (outer faces shaded; coloured by material, a
                         vertex or cell attribute, or aspect ratio,
                         with a legend; planar meshes seen down their
                         normal; octrees: leaves only)
  pictures.py            the picture run (extra dumps) and the list of
                         pictures of a test; pictures.txt says what to
                         draw for which test
  compare_outputs.py     every output file against the stored truth
  extra_checks.py        checks of our own for some tests (pset)
  notes.txt              the explanation shown on a test's row
  truth/                 truth files of our own, where LaGriT's
                         reference/ copy is out of date (none needed
                         yet; see compare_outputs.py)
  known.txt              documented failures: "test | why"
  work/                  where the tests ran (outputs; deleted and
                         remade by every run)
  logs/                  per test: <test>.stdout, <test>.outx3dgen;
                         tests.txt (exit status, seconds), run_info.txt,
                         table.txt, summary.html

THE CHECKS

  PASS needs all five:
  same      the log equals reference/outx3dgen by LaGriT's own rules,
            with LaGriT's own code (testlagrit/diff.py from the source
            tree): blank lines, comment lines and the banner skipped;
            lines compared word by word; numbers within an ABSOLUTE
            0.001; anything else exactly. Two corrections of ours:
            1. LaGriT's diff.py MISSES every isolated one-line
               difference. It asks for a unified diff with no context;
               a block of one changed line then has the header
               "@@ -39 +39 @@" (one line each), which diff.py reads as
               0 lines, so it compares nothing. Found Oct 16 2026: a
               reference total volume changed from 1.0 to 1.1 passed
               LaGriT's own runtests.py. This checker finds the blocks
               itself and still records diff.py's verdict; a test
               diff.py would have passed is marked "LaGriT's diff.py
               misses it". (Worth reporting upstream.)
            2. A negative zero equals zero ("-0.0000000j" against
               "0.0000000j"): the sign of a printed zero depends on the
               compiler and platform. Five tests differ only in this
               (addmesh_doublemesh, refine_recon_quality, setpts,
               smooth, surface: the unit vectors of planes).
  exit      lagrit's exit status is 0 (a crash is 139)
  finished  the log has "LaGriT successfully completed" (every
            reference has it)
  files     every output file the test's reference/ folder keeps (the
            stored truth: LaGriT keeps it there but its runtests.py
            never compares it; user, Oct 17 2026) equals the file the
            test wrote: meshes as meshes (vertices within 1e-6 of the
            mesh size, the same cells in any order, attributes within
            a relative 1e-6), text word by word (numbers within a
            relative 1e-6; date lines such as a .stor header skipped),
            anything else byte for byte. truth/<test>/<file> overrides
            reference/ where that copy is out of date. Reference files
            the deck no longer writes are listed as "not written" (not
            a failure). Oct 17: 157 files compared, all equal.
  own       our own check, for a test in extra_checks.py (pset: every
            written vertex set file read back, ASCII and binary, and
            compared with the sets' definitions on the mesh)
  LaGriT's runtests.py makes none of the last four checks.

  KNOWN  a test in known.txt that fails, as documented there
  FIXED  a test in known.txt that now passes: remove its line
  FAIL   anything else. The runner exits nonzero on any FAIL.

